From 96260a3e5987e4189e9267f0f2a52004353f7c01 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nipuna H Herath <nipunaherath@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 11:01:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 02/10] CUDA/HIP: one kernel for Inkling 2's per-head attention
 prep of one token

Between the attention projections and flash attention a generated token ran
six kernels per layer pass, each a few vectors of 16-128 values: Q * w ->
L2 clip, K L2 clip, V RMS norm, SET_ROWS of K and of V into the cache, R * w
-> L2 clip -> scale, and in the full-attention layers Q * tau and R * tau.
They now run as one kernel with a warp per head row (24 Q, 8 K, 8 V, 24 R),
which writes K and V straight into the F16 cache rows the SET_ROWS name (the
row index read on the device, so it captures in a graph). The reductions
follow the unfused kernels exactly, including the four-consecutive-values
order of the vectorized rms_norm, so the output is bit-identical.

The F32 K and V results are not written when nothing but the SET_ROWS reads
them: unfused they die before R is computed, so the allocator may give R
their memory, and a kernel writing all three at once would race. Outputs
that are written are also checked against each other (fusion refused on
overlap), besides against every input. tau is now expanded at the start of
the graph, so none of its nodes land inside the first full-attention layer's
pattern.

R9700 Inkling-2-Mini UD-Q8_K_XL: 2,138 -> 1,876 kernels per generated token
(all 48 layer passes fuse); tg128 53.9 -> 56.1 tok/s (+4.0%). KLD against
the previous commit, wiki.test.raw -c 512, -ub 512 and -ub 1: 0, 100% same
top token. New test-backend-ops case ATTN_PREP, with and without tau.
